A||||11||myblack86GT||Tires and Lowering Springs||||5||
Z||000000||myblack86GT||09-15-2000||06:28 PM||||I am needing a set of new tires for my 86GT with 15in stock rims. I currently have P205/60R15 on the front and P215/60R15 on the back. They are Firestones. I would like to get some good handling for wet and dry conditions. I would like to know what you guy reccomend for this car. Also, I am looking at putting on some 1" lowering springs on front and rear. Should I be looking at maybe bigger then 1"? Like maybe 1 1/2". I saw somone with 2" springs on his Fiero and he said he was having trouble with the front tires hitting the wheelwells on bumpy roads. I dont want anything bigger then 1 1/2", or should I just go with the 1"? Will they work with the factory shocks? I dont know anything about this, so Im just making sure. Thanks. <P>One more thing, are those Goodyear Aquatreads any good?||||reg||5
Z||000001||myblack86GT||09-15-2000||11:10 PM||||Anyone?||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||09-15-2000||11:23 PM||||I have 195/70R14 on mine. Right now I have some tires from national tire and battery. Responce RST touring 2000, I think. They are made by cooper according to NTB. They handle very well on my 87SC. Decent in the rain as well. The 15" version should be very similar.<P>I've used coopers on other things with pleasnt results. This set was around $375 mounted and balanced. (I have goodyear on the caravan, they actually cost less. I forget which ones thay are right now. Too dark & crappy to go look. Those are nice tires to. Gave a major boost to the truck. also 14" but 205/70)<P>I haven't touched a firestone tire since the 721 recall years ago. I didn't like their additude then and like it less now.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Oreif||09-16-2000||01:10 AM||||I lowered my car 1" and put a set of 15" GT wheels with BFGoorich Euro T/A's 225/50R15.<BR>They handle great wet or dry. They also are sized correctly for the wheel wells. <P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LOGO3.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||2birds||09-16-2000||01:33 AM||||I have BF Goodrich Comp T/A HR4, 225/60/15 in back, and 215/60/15 in front, plus Suspension Technique springs all around.  Decent tires in the rain, IMHO.  The fronts would scrape the inner fender liner at full lock, for the first 10,000 miles or so.  Use stock sizes or stock springs, and no rubbing.  Stock type Monroe Gasmatic shocks and struts.  Roughly 1.5" drop, I think.  Check the North Florida Fieros Website for a pic.  Mine's red and silver (John from South Georgia).||||reg||1
Z||000005||myblack86GT||09-16-2000||09:01 AM||||Yeah, Im with ya about the Firestones, but they were on in when I got the car. Im wondering too, on the Fiero Store web site, they have a KONI Racing Suspension Kit. I was wondering if it is really worth the money. Its right around $1000. It includes 4 1in lowering springs, 2 ajustable shock and 2 ajustable struts, replacment front sway bar, and addon read sway bar, and mounting hardware. Also Iv been hearing about the coil-over suspension kit. I use the car as a daily driver and occasionally race some out-of-towers with high-performance car, or so they think. Would this coil-over be somthing I should look into, or just stick with the shocks and possibly lowering springs? Sorry, but one more question: Could somone explain to me what the numbers for the tire size mean? (EX. P215/60R15) All I know is the last number is the diam. Thanks||||reg||1
Z||000006||-=shame=-||09-16-2000||10:10 AM||||I was told (not by a trustable source) that the other numbers are the thickness of the tire and the height of the side wall. As far as lowering it, I think you guys are nuts! But maybe thats because I'm 6'2". I have enough trouble as it is.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Standard||09-16-2000||12:33 PM||||What the numbers mean:<BR>ex. P215/60R14 - <P>P = Passenger car tire<P>215 = 'Section width', width of the tread in millimeters.  Doesn't include any part of the sidewall.<P>60 = Aspect ratio, reflects the height of the sidewall.  The 60 means the sidewall is 60% as tall as the 215 mm tread width.  Basically, the smaller this number the thinner the sidewall<P>R = Radial<P>14 = Size of rim in inches the tire fits on.<P>Sometimes there will be another letter before the R, which is the speed rating. It reflects the maximum sustained speed of the tires.<BR>S = 112 mph<BR>T = 118 <BR>U = 124 <BR>H = 130<BR>V = 149<BR>W = 168<BR>Y = 186<BR>Z = 186+<P>Hope this helps   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Mike -<BR>Black 86.5 GT 5 Spd, 150,000 miles and counting!        <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/BURNOUT.gif">     <BR>Red 84 Sport Coupe - winter donut maker<p>[This message has been edited by Standard (edited 09-16-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||btoth||09-16-2000||12:39 PM||||i was using BF Goodrich Comp T/A HR4 but switch to the VR4's... they are very good all weather tires but were kinda expensive because they are V rated.  i like the little bit harder ride of the V rated myself.<P>i don't remember where i got this but try <A HREF="http://www.miata.net/garage/tirecalc.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.miata.net/garage/tirecalc.html</A>  to compare sizes.<p>[This message has been edited by btoth (edited 09-16-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||Oreif||09-16-2000||03:18 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by -=shame=-:<BR><B> As far as lowering it, I think you guys are nuts! But maybe thats because I'm 6'2". I have enough trouble as it is.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>I don't have a problem with my lowered Fiero, I am 6'1". Lowering the car 1" isn't that big of a deal in terms of access in and out. The only person to complain about getting in and out was my Wife. When wearing a short skirt, I get a real nice peep show!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P><p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 09-16-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000010||3400Fiero||09-16-2000||08:44 PM||||I have lowered my 88 formula 1.5 in all around and am 6' 3" !   I did it by cutting the springs, and have been thinking of replacing them with the eibach lowering springs.  Anybody have any experience with them - ie the ones from ACE?<BR><P>------------------<BR>1988 Fiero 3400DOHC<BR>||||reg||14
Z||000011||Phil||09-16-2000||08:50 PM||||I have ACE Eibachs on my Formula -lowers about 1.5" no problems but it does ride stiffer than stock and on real lumpy ground does make some clunking noises other than that just fine. Also running Konis||||reg||1
